===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A shaking of the [[ground]], caused by [[volcanic]] activity or movement around [[geologic]] [[fault]]s. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|passage=Her alablaster brest she soft did kis, / Which all that while shee felt to pant and quake, / As it an '''Earth-quake''' were: at last she thus bespake.|3|2}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1869|author={{w|Alfred Russel Wallace}}|title={{w|The Malay Archipelago}}|publisher=Macmillan and Co.|year_published=|volume=II|location=London|page=9|passage=I had just awoke at gun-fire (5 A.M.), when suddenly the thatch began to rustle and shake as if an army of cats were galloping over it, and immediately afterwards my bed shook too, so that for an instant I imagined myself back in New Guinea, in my fragile house, which shook when an old cock went to roost on the ridge; but remembering that I was now on a solid earthen floor, I said to myself, "Why, it's an '''earthquake'''," and lay still in the pleasing expectation of another shock; but none came, and this was the only '''earthquake''' I ever felt in Ternate.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Declan Walsh|journal=The Guardian|date=6 Oct 2006
|passage=Last year's '''earthquake''' crushed his house, his livelihood and very nearly his leg, he said, pointing to a plastered limb that refuses to heal.}}
# {{lb|en|planetary geology}} Such a quake specifically occurring on the planet Earth, as opposed to other celestial bodies. {{defdate|from 20th c.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1988|author=Jürgen Oberst; Yosio Nakamura|chapter=A seismic risk for the lunar base|title=The Second Conference on Lunar Bases and Space Activities of the 21st Century|volume=1|pages=231–233|publisher=NASA
|passage=Since the response of some man-made structures to the ground motion near the epicenter is highly dependent on frequency, a significant difference in potential damage to the structures is expected between '''earthquakes''' and moonquakes.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2006|author=Bruce A. Bolt|title=Earthquakes, Fifth Edition|passage=The wave patterns, too, are strikingly different: The secondary (S) waves and surface waves on lunar seismograms are not generally as clearly defined and distinct as are those of '''earthquakes'''.}}
# {{lb|en|figuratively}} A [[sudden]] and [[intense]] [[upheaval]]; a [[severely]] [[disruptive]] event.
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2019-07-11|author=John Thornhill|title=Does tech threaten to rerun the worst of the Industrial Revolution?|journal=w:Financial Times|url=https://www.ft.com/content/f1b84f22-a25b-11e9-974c-ad1c6ab5efd1|passage=As we have seen, economic '''earthquakes''' often result in political shockwaves. {{...}} He highlights a correlation between those states with the highest robot density and those states that unexpectedly swung behind Donald Trump in the 2016 presidential election, namely Michigan, Pennsylvania and Wisconsin.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en-GB|author=Jon Henley|title=Leo Varadkar steps down as Irish prime minister in shock move|work=The Guardian|date=2024-03-20|url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2024/mar/20/leo-varadkar-step-downs-as-irish-prime-minister-in-shock-move|issn=0261-3077|passage=Leo Varadkar has announced he is standing down as Ireland’s prime minister and also giving up his role as leader of the Fine Gael party in the ruling coalition, in a surprise move described by pundits as a “political '''earthquake'''” for the country.}}

