===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*dʰrewgʰ-|id=deceive}}
{{etymon|en|:inh|enm:drem|tree=+}}
From {{inh|en|enm|dream}}, {{m|enm|dreem}}, {{m|enm|dreeme}}, {{m|enm|drem}}, {{m|enm|dreme}}, {{m|enm|dræm}}, from {{inh|en|ang|drēam}}, {{m|ang|drēm}}, {{m|ang|drīm|t=joy; melody, music; frenzy}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*draum}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*draumaz|t=dream}}, from earlier {{m|gem-pro||*draugmaz}}, from {{inh|en|ine-pro||*dʰrowgʰ-mos}}, from {{m|ine-pro|*dʰrewgʰ-|t=to deceive}}.

The sense of "dream", though not attested in Old English, may still have been present (compare {{cog|osx|drōm|t=bustle, revelry, jubilation", also "dream}}), and was undoubtedly reinforced later in Middle English by {{cog|non|draumr|t=dream}}, from same Proto-Germanic root.
{{col-top|2|cog}}
Cognate with {{cog|sco|dreme|t=dream}}, {{cog|stq|Droom|t=dream}}, {{cog|fy|dream|t=dream}}, {{cog|nl|droom|t=dream}}, {{cog|de|Traum|t=dream}}, {{cog|li|Droum|t=dream}}, {{cog|lb|Dram|t=dream}}, {{cog|yi|טרוים|t=dream}}, {{cog|da,nb|drøm|t=dream}}, {{cog|fo|dreymur|t=dream}}, {{cog|is|draumur|t=dream}}, {{cog|nn|draum}}, {{m|nn|drøm|t=dream}}, {{cog|sv|dröm|t=dream}}; also {{cog|br|drouk|t=bad; evil}}, {{cog|kw|drog|t=bad; evil; invalid; nasty; wicked; wrong}}, {{cog|ga|droch-|t=bad; ill-}}, {{cog|gv|drogh|t=bad; evil}}, {{cog|gd|droch|t=bad}}, {{cog|cy|drwg|t=bad; naughty}}, {{cog|ae|𐬛𐬭𐬎𐬲𐬀𐬌𐬙𐬌|t=to deceive, mislead}}, {{cog|xbc|λρωγο|t=lie; falsehood}}, {{cog|bal|دروگ|tr=darog|t=lie; falsehood}}, {{cog|ckb|درۆ|t=lie}}, {{cog|kmr|derew|t=lie}}, {{cog|ps|دروغ|tr=droğ|t=lie; falsehood}}, {{cog|fa|دُروغ|tr=doruġ / durōġ|t=lie; falsehood}}, {{cog|sa|द्रुह्यति|t=to hurt; to bear malice or hatred; to be a foe or rival}}.
{{col-bottom}}
The verb is from {{inh|en|enm|dreamen}}, {{m|enm|dreme}}, {{m|enm|dremen}}, {{m|enm|dreomen}}, possibly (see below) from {{inh|en|ang|drēman}}, {{m|ang|drīeman}}, {{m|ang|drȳman|t=to rejoice; to play an instrument joyously; to sing a song joyfully}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*draumijan}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*draumijaną|t=to be dream}}, from the noun. 
{{col-top|2|cog}}
Cognate with {{cog|frr|drem}}, {{m|frr|driime|t=to dream}}, {{cog|stq|drööme|t=to dream}}, {{cog|fy|dreame}}, {{m|fy|dreamje|t=to dream}}, {{cog|bar|dramma}}, {{m|bar|trama|t=to dream}}, {{cog|gmw-cfr|drööme|t=to dream}}, {{cog|cim|intruamen|truamen|t=to dream}}, {{cog|nl|dromen|t=to dream}}, {{cog|de|träumen|t=to dream}}, {{cog|nds|draimen|t=to dream}}, {{cog|lb|dreemen|t=to dream}}, {{cog|yi|טרוימען|t=to dream}}, {{cog|da,nb|drømme|t=to dream}}, {{cog|fo|droyma|t=to dream}}, {{cog|is|dreyma|t=to dream}}, {{cog|nn|drømma}}, {{m|cog|drømme}}, {{m|nn|drøyma}}, {{m|nn|drøyme|t=to dream}}, {{cog|sv|drömma|t=to dream, muse}}.
{{col-bottom}}
{{col-top|2|more details}}
The derivation from Old English ''drēam'' is controversial, since the word itself is only attested in writing in its meaning of “joy, mirth, musical sound”. Possibly there was a separate word ''drēam'' meaning “images seen while sleeping”, which was avoided in literature due to potential confusion with the “joy” sense. Otherwise, the modern sense must have been borrowed from another Germanic language, most probably Old Norse.<ref>{{R:Online Etymology Dictionary}}.</ref> Since this is the common sense in all Germanic languages outside the British isles, a spontaneous development from “joy, mirth” to “dream” in Middle English is hardly conceivable. In {{m+|osx|-}}, the cognate {{m|osx|drōm}} did mean “dream”, but was a rare word.

Attested words for “sleeping vision” in Old English, both of which appeared in ''The Dream of the Rood'', were {{m|ang|mǣting}} (Middle English {{m|enm|mæte}}, {{m|enm|mete}}), from an unclear source, and {{m|ang|swefn}} (Modern {{cog|en|sweven}}), from {{cog|gem-pro|*swefnaz}}, from Proto-Indo-European {{m|ine-pro|*swepno-}}, {{m|ine-pro|*swep-}}; compare {{cog|grc|ὕπνος||sleep}}.
{{col-bottom}}

