##* {{quote-book|en|year=1841  |title=Intermarriage|author=Alexander Walker |page=325 |ISBN=|passage=As an instance: amongst a '''draft''' of young hounds from Earl F itzwilliam's was one, of whom Will Deane, his huntsman, made this remark in his letter, 'that he could not guess at Lord Foley's dislike to the hound called Glider, then sent, which was of the best blood in the country, being got by Mr. Meynell's Glider out of Lord Fitzwilliam's Blossom, and was moreover the most promising young hound he had ever entered;...}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1904 |title=Sessional papers. Inventory control record 1 - Volume 42 |author=Great Britain. Parliament. House of Commons |page=83 |ISBN=|passage=These '''drafts''' left between the 17th March and 28th April. After this there was no regular system of artillery '''drafts'''. A '''draft''' of 80 was detailed to take charge of horses on board ship as all cavalry '''drafts''' were for the time exhausted, and this '''draft''' sailed on the 30th June. }}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1982 |title=Letters of John Maxwell, Superintendent of Government Stock|author=John Maxwell; Brian Edmund Lloyd; Bertha Mac Smith |page=206 |ISBN=|passage=The Complaint about the Beef lately furnished for the use of the Troops &c at Parramatta, is not without foundation; when I commenced supplying the several Stations with animal food in January last, — a '''Draft''' of very fat Oxen was brought from Wellington for that purpose, — that '''draft''' has met the consumption of several Stations until now. — the Cattle lately remaining fell very much off in condition, though I certainly consider they were not inferior to a great deal of Beef, I have seen taken from Contractors.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1993 |title=A History of the British Cavalry 1816-1919: Volume 2: 1851-1871|author=Lord Anglesey |page= |ISBN=|passage=An earlier '''draft''' of horses for the 4th Light Dragoons, however,was very different.}}
