===Etymology 1===
[[File:Ceremony at Capel Moriah 07.JPG|thumb|A European dragon. (Some notable countries are England, France, and Germany)]]
[[File:Fengdu Ghost City - dragon.JPG|thumb|An East Asian dragon. (Some notable countries are China, Mongolia, and Japan)]]
[[File:Flag of Wales.svg|thumb|The red dragon (sense 1.1) (depicted in the Flag of Wales) is the national symbol of Wales.]]
[[File:Miaoli-County Taiwan Quanhua-Temple-02.jpg|thumb|Sculptures of a pair of East Asian dragons (sense 1.2) on top of a temple.]]

{{ety|en|id=mythical creature|:inh|enm:dragoun|tree=1}}
From {{inh|en|enm|dragoun}}, borrowed from {{der|en|fro|dragon}}, from {{der|en|la|draco|dracō(n)}}, from {{der|en|grc|δράκων||a serpent of huge size, a python, a dragon}}, probably from {{m|grc|δέρκομαι||to see clearly}}. Displaced {{inh|en|ang|wyrm}}, whence modern {{l|en|worm}}. Mostly displaced {{inh|en|ang|draca}} (whence modern {{m|en|drake|id=dragon}})—from the same Latin source, as are {{doublet|en|Draco|Dracon|dracone|dragoon|notext=1}}.

