##* {{quote-text|en|year=c. 1900|author=w:Edith Nesbit|title=s:The Last of the Dragons
|passage=But as every well-brought-up prince was expected to kill a '''dragon''', and rescue a princess, the '''dragons''' grew fewer and fewer till it was often quite hard for a princess to find a dragon to be rescued from.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1961|author=w:Norma Lorre Goodrich|title=The Medieval Myths|location=New York|publisher=The New American Library|chapter=Beowulf|page=41|passage=Before daylight, when the '''dragon''' flew home to sleep, he had burned up the hall and even the throne of the Geatish king.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1999|author=w:Seamus Heaney|title=w:Beowulf|publisher=Faber and Faber|chapter=|location=London|page=98|passage=They pitched the '''dragon''' / over the cliff-top, let tide's flow / and backwash take the treasure-minder.}}
