#* {{quote-book|en|year=1972|author={{w|Frederick Valentine Atkinson|F. V. Atkinson}}|title=Multiparameter '''Eigenvalue''' Problems, Volume I: Matrices and Compact Operators|pageurl=https://books.google.com.au/books?id=VRYSzh33uPMC&pg=PR10&dq=%22eigenvalue%22%7C%22eigenvalues%22&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwjP97SwuO_XAhUBG5AKHbVzCpAQ6AEI4gEwIQ#v=onepage&q=%22eigenvalue%22%7C%22eigenvalues%22&f=false|page=x|publisher=Academic Press
|passage=In the extension, one associates '''eigenvalues''', sets of scalars, with arrays of matrices by considering the singularity of linear combinations of the matrices in the various rows, involving the same coefficients in each case. Attention to this area was called in the early 1920's by R. D. Carmichael, who pointed out in addition the enormous variety of mixed '''eigenvalue''' problems with several parameters.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2000|translator=Hinne Hettema|author=w:John von Neumann; {{w|Eugene Wigner|E. Wigner}}|chapter=On the Behaviour of '''Eigenvalues''' in Adiabatic Processes|year_published=1929|editor=Hinne Hettema|title=Quantum Chemistry: Classic Scientific Papers|publisher=World Scientific|pageurl=https://books.google.com.au/books?id=qsidHRJmUoIC&pg=PA25&dq=%22eigenvalue%22%7C%22eigenvalues%22&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwjP97SwuO_XAhUBG5AKHbVzCpAQ6AEI1wIwNg#v=onepage&q=%22eigenvalue%22%7C%22eigenvalues%22&f=false|page=25
|passage=For many quantum-mechanical problems it is important to investigate the change of '''eigenvalues''' and eigenfunctions with the continuous change of one or more parameters. The case in which one knows the '''eigenvalues''' and eigenfunctions for two special values of the parameters, and is interested in the region in between is particularly interesting.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2005|author=Leonid D. Akulenko; Sergei V. Nesterov|title=High-Precision Methods in '''Eigenvalue''' Problems and Their Applications|publisher=CRC Press (Chapman & Hall)|url=https://books.google.com.au/books?id=kKjMSwgH-noC&pg=PA1&dq=%22eigenvalue%22%7C%22eigenvalues%22&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwjP97SwuO_XAhUBG5AKHbVzCpAQ6AEIwgEwHA#v=onepage&q=%22eigenvalue%22%7C%22eigenvalues%22&f=false|page=1
|passage=Problems that require an investigation of '''eigenvalues''' and eigenfunctions arise in connection with numerous topics in mechanics, the theory of vibrations and stability, hydrodynamics, elasticity, acoustics, electrodynamics, quantum mechanics, etc.}}
#* {{quote-journal|1=en|title=Lesson 12|work=w:University College London|url=https://www.ucl.ac.uk/~ucahmdl/LessonPlans/Lesson12.pdf|page=1|passage=To find all the '''eigenvalues''' of A, solve the characteristic equation. ¶ For each '''eigenvalue''' λ, to find the corresponding set of eigenvectors, solve the linear system of equations {{...}}|accessdate=22 March 2025|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20250321184114/https://www.ucl.ac.uk/~ucahmdl/LessonPlans/Lesson12.pdf|archivedate=21 March 2025}}

====Usage notes====
* {{m|en||Eigenvalue}} is the standard term in English, displacing the older {{m|en|proper value}}.
